@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
' '   description:  Order lifecycle: New to Delivered with a Cancelled exit and guarded transitions.
'   usecase:      Teach 6-8 states, guarded transitions, and entry/do/exit behaviors.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       e-commerce
' ============================================================================
title Example State Machine - Medium Order Lifecycle

[*] --> New : create_order

state "New" as New
state "Paid" as Paid
state "Picking" as Picking
state "Shipped" as Shipped
state "Delivered" as Delivered
state "Cancelled" as Cancelled

' NORMAL happy-path transitions.
New --> Paid : payment_received[amount >= total]
Paid --> Picking : begin_picking
Picking --> Shipped : ship
Shipped --> Delivered : deliver_confirmed

' GUARDED transitions into Cancelled fire from multiple sources.
New --> Cancelled : cancel[within_cancellation_window]
Paid --> Cancelled : cancel[refund_approved]
Picking --> Cancelled : cancel[items_not_yet_dispatched]

Delivered --> [*]
Cancelled --> [*]

' STATE BEHAVIORS: each state may declare internal behaviors that run at
' different times without themselves being transitions:
'   entry / ...   runs ONCE when the state is entered
'   do    / ...   runs CONTINUOUSLY while in the state (until a transition fires)
'   exit  / ...   runs ONCE when leaving the state
' We demonstrate these on the Picking state.

state Picking {
  Picking : entry / reserve_stock()
  Picking : do / pick_items_from_shelf()
  Picking : exit / release_unpicked_reservations()
}

' TEACHING NOTES:
'   - Guards make the SAME event route differently depending on state/context.
'     e.g. "cancel" is only valid while cancellation is still possible.
'   - entry/do/exit let a state encapsulate its own bookkeeping.
'   - do-behaviors are interruptible; they stop the instant a transition fires.
'   - A single event (e.g. cancel) can be valid in several states, each with
'     its own guard and target.
@enduml
